{
  "ID": "3.2",
  "Context/Background": "To find novel treatments and drug combinations that could effectively target colorectal cancer cells (CRCs) and lower the probability of disease relapse, we designed multiple different combinations between cisplatin, cannabidiol (CBD), and intermittent serum starvation (ISS) on colorectal cancer cell lines. We analyzed differentially expressed genes and affected pathways in colorectal cancer cell lines to understand further the potential molecular mechanisms behind the treatments and their interactions.",
  "Prompt": "Based on the results of the differential expression analysis of CBD vs DMSO in colorectal cancer cells, run a pathway overrepresentation analysis for statistically significant genes (up and down combined) against the Reactome database. Then, provide a discussion of any relevant affected pathways, highlighting important up and down regulated genes in each.  Focus on any mechanisms by which CBD may alter cancer related pathways.",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "openai",
  "llm_model": "o4-mini",
  "run_date": "2025-12-14 20:44:55",
  "elapsed_seconds": 132.0,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "Pathway Over-Representation Analysis (Reactome) was performed on the 2 051 genes differentially expressed between CBD- and DMSO-treated colorectal cancer (CRC) cells (padj < 0.05; ORA via Reactome_Pathways_2024).  Below are the most highly enriched cancer-relevant pathways, the key up- and down-regulated genes within each, and a brief mechanistic interpretation of how CBD may exert anti-tumor effects in CRC.\n\n1.  Oncogene-Induced Senescence  \n    • Adjusted P-value: 1.30 × 10⁻⁵  \n    • Overlap (16/35 genes): RB1, CDKN2A, ETS1, ETS2, CDK6, TFDP1, UBB, CDK4, UBC, ID1, MDM2, MAPK1, MDM4, TP53, TNRC6A, TNRC6B  \n    • Up-regulated highlights: MDM2, CDK6, ID1  \n    • Down-regulated highlights: CDKN2A, RB1  \n    → CBD treatment appears to engage a senescence program by tipping the balance of CDK–RB and p53/MDM2 regulation, consistent with enforced cell‐cycle exit.\n\n2.  Transcriptional Regulation by RUNX3  \n    • Adjusted P-value: 1.02 × 10⁻⁶  \n    • Overlap (29/86 genes): YAP1, NOTCH1, MAML2, TCF7, RBPJ, FOXO3, CCND1, UBB, PSMD2, UBC, WWTR1, SMAD3, KRAS, MDM2, TP53, …  \n    • Up-regulated highlights: FOXO3, MDM2, CCND1  \n    • Down-regulated highlights: SMAD3, KRAS  \n    → Modulation of RUNX3 targets suggests CBD may rewire developmental and stress-responsive transcriptional networks to favor growth arrest and apoptosis.\n\n3.  FOXO-Mediated Transcription of Cell Cycle Genes  \n    • Adjusted P-value: 6.10 × 10⁻⁴  \n    • Overlap (9/17 genes): SMAD2, BTG1, CDKN1B (p27), SMAD3, GADD45A, CAV1, CCNG2, FOXO3, KLF4  \n    • Up-regulated highlights: CDKN1B, GADD45A, FOXO3  \n    • Down-regulated highlights: SMAD2, SMAD3  \n    → Upregulation of CDKN1B and GADD45A, canonical FOXO targets, points to CBD-induced G₁ arrest and DNA repair‐linked checkpoints.\n\n4.  Downregulation of TGF-β Receptor Signaling  \n    • Adjusted P-value: 8.01 × 10⁻⁴  \n    • Overlap (11/26 genes): PPP1R15A, SMAD2, PPP1CC, XPO1, SMAD3, UBB, SMURF2, UBC, TGFBR1, SMAD7, TGFBR2  \n    • Up-regulated highlights: SMAD7, PPP1R15A  \n    • Down-regulated highlights: SMAD2, TGFBR1, TGFBR2  \n    → CBD may blunt the pro-tumorigenic arm of TGF-β signaling by decreasing receptor/SMAD2/3 while inducing inhibitory SMAD7, shifting cells toward growth arrest.\n\n5.  Toll-Like Receptor (TLR) 3 and TLR4 Cascades  \n    • Adjusted P-values: TLR3 Cascade 9.03 × 10⁻⁶; TRIF-mediated TLR4 1.30 × 10⁻⁵  \n    • Overlap (31/106 and 31/110 genes): ATF2, RELA, IRAK1, JUN, FOS, NFKB2, MAPK1, IRF7, TRAF3, TRAF6, …  \n    • Up-regulated highlights: RELA (NF-κB), IRF7, ATF2  \n    • Down-regulated highlights: none clear-cut  \n    → Enrichment of innate immune signaling suggests CBD may modulate inflammatory networks in CRC cells, potentially altering tumor–immune interactions.\n\nMechanistic summary:  \nBy simultaneously engaging senescence and cell-cycle checkpoint pathways (Oncogene-Induced Senescence, FOXO/CDKN1B, RUNX3), inhibiting TGF-β pro-survival signals (loss of SMAD2/3, TGFBR1/2 while upregulating SMAD7), and activating innate immunomodulatory cascades (TLR3/4), CBD appears to impose a multi-layered brake on colorectal cancer cell proliferation and survival. Key up-regulated effectors MDM2, CDK6, CDKN1B/p27, FOXO3 and down-regulated drivers SMAD2/3 and TGF-β receptors together provide a coherent mechanism for CBD-induced growth arrest and potential enhancement of anti-tumor immunity in CRC.",
